me that all of the  advantages that procalcitonin offers  would extend to this kind of a strategy  as well and perhaps even better there is  a rapid test that's being developed but  is not available as of yet another thing  a few other things to point out  in terms of weaknesses is that there is  some incorporation bias meaning that  that cases were determined to be  bacterial or viral through an  adjudication process and that the  adjudication process incorporated  c-reactive protein and so that can be to  some bias when CRP is both used to  assign your reference standard and is  also part of the test you're evaluating  the the test itself there was some  demonstration of how it performed in  patients with non-infectious illness but  by and large it hasn't really been  evaluated in that particular population  and then indeterminate or intermediate  results that middle group which  comprised about 15% of their test  population were discarded from the  analysis and were not included in their  calculations of sensitivity and  specificity so the numbers for  sensitivity and specificity are likely  to be lower when you actually evaluate  them on  every patient that was I was tested but  nevertheless from these these values  that even when you take that into  account offers are significant  improvement over the current state of  affairs and is equivalent if not better  than procalcitonin so certainly moving  in the right direction now this this  need to and motivation to generate  bacterial versus viral discriminating  tests was one that was highlighted in an  a prize a challenge that was developed  and supported by the NIH CDC and Barda  and in this case there were ten  semi-finalists that were selected for  the first phase of competition there are  a total of three phases of competition  over spread out over a few years and as  a twenty million dollar prize that  ultimately supports these different  participants and there were a number of  different strategies some of them were  focused on detection of antimicrobial  resistance but some of them were focused  on discrimination of bacterial and viral  ideologies and I'll highlight some work  that that we did ourselves that was one  of those ten groups that were that were  selected and it was developing host gene  expression to classify viral and  bacterial infection and so this is a bit  of a departure from current approaches  because it focuses on the patient's gene  expression to help discriminate  ideologies the underlying premise is  that there are different immune  responses depending on whether or not a  patient is fighting off a bacterial  viral fungal infection parasitic  infection or no infection and we started  off this lecture focusing on pathogen  detection assays we can also measure the  immune response and knowing that there  are are unique features of the immune  response we can then extrapolate from  that and determine whether or not it is  a bacterial viral or some other pathogen  causing illness so the way that we did  this in a paper that was published now a  couple of years ago was we generated  three independent gene expression based  classifiers one of them discriminated  patients with bacterial acute  respiratory illness  versus alternatives where the  alternatives were either viral infection  or no infection  a viral signature and then a signature  that discriminated patients with  infection from those without we then  applied all three classifiers to a group  of about two hundred and seventy-three  subjects and we assumed that whatever  the problem the highest probability was  because again there are three  probabilities that of a bacterial  etiology a viral etiology or  non-infectious etiology whatever the  highest probability was that determined  the class and that's demonstrated here  our reference standard was a clinical  adjudication which is represented by the  color that dark blue purple color the  are those who were determined to be  bacterial and if the tests identified  them as having a bacterial infection it  grouped them in the lower left part of  the triangle and so if you were viral  those were grouped in the top portion of  the triangle and if the test predicted a  patient as having a non infectious  illness those were in the bottom right  portion of the triangle so bacteria  bottom left  viral top non-infectious bottom right  and what you would expect to see or hope  to see and what you do see is that the  viral cases are clustering at the top  where you would expect them to the  bacterial cases are clustering at the  bottom left and the the non-infectious  cases on the bottom right test isn't  perfect you do see some colors that are  appearing in other areas in some of  those cases we went back and and and  realized that our clinical adjudication  which is by far not perfect may very  well have been wrong given the new  information that was provided by the  patient's gene expression profile but  nevertheless this this was a promising  approach we compared this to  procalcitonin and found that if the  question was does the patient have a  viral infection or not that a gene  expression approach was was pretty much  the same as procalcitonin but if the  question was does this patient have a  bacterial infection or not that's when  we started to see improvements and in  particular if the question was does the  patient have an infection of any kind or  not that's what we saw the greatest  ability  of these gene expression signatures over  procalcitonin to make the right call  well these signatures are all well and  good but ultimately what we're trying to  do is to get to a point where we have a  test available for use and so what's  happened since then is some development  work with with one company with two  companies I'll tell you about those very  briefly one of them is with bio fire  which we mentioned earlier makes nucleic  acid amplification tests for pathogen  detection assays the test itself is  relatively simple to use results in  about 45 minutes  and can work direct from blood and these  were results that were presented at ASM  in 2017 this is a preliminary version of  a bio fire based test that measures the  patient's gene expression signature in  the top group or the viral results in  the bottom group of the bacterial  results the left panel is a box plot  that highlights patients with viral  infection versus non viral theology's  and that includes bacterial infections  as well as patients who are sick without  infection and the numbers are hard to  see but the on the top right is a ROC  curve and they're a perfect test would  give you an area under the curve of one  and a useless test a completely random  test would give you an area under the  curve 0.5 and we're seeing an area under  the curve of 0.92 which is excellent in  terms of infectious disease biomarkers  that's along the lines of what you might  see with troponin or BNP for example in  cardiovascular disease in the bottom  group you see those cases of bacterial  infection being discriminated from non  bacterial infection the non bacterial  group was comprised of viral infection  as well as non infectious to illness and  there you see an AUC 0.87 a little bit  lower than that a viral infection still  again very good compared to what's  currently available this is what that  looked like or just a random selection  of patients that were tested those with  an adjudicated viral infection or in  that leftmost group and they have high  probabilities of having a viral  infection a low  abilities a bacterial infection you see  the opposite result with the next group  in the bacterial cases and then if you  were sick without infection and healthy  controls and both ended up with low  probabilities of both viral and  bacterial infections another company  that these gene expression assays have  been developed with is Q bella q bella  is not on the market but they're  generating a test that performs direc